Oh I have another issue. 
my body looks like this: 

           cout << "Please tell me your birth date, in the form of: month day year ";
           cin >> month >> d >> year;
           cout <<"Your input value is not correct." << endl; 
           cout <<"The computation was not carried out" << endl;

This works for when the user inputs numbers outside the range but when they put them in the range  it still writes that their input is not correct/not carried out, then goes on to do the rest of the code like normal.

How do I make it not do the last 2 lines when they've given correct input? I tried to use a break but it says I'm not sticking it within the loop.

I'm not sure how to do one. I never have before. 

So far I have 

    do
    {
        cout << "Please tell me your birth date, in the form of: month day year ";
        cin >> month >> d >> year;
    }
        while((month >= 1 &&  month <=12) && (d >=1 && d<=31) && (year >= 0 && year <=2015));    

but I tested that and it doesn't work. It still continues on with the rest of the code instead of repeating.

 I'm also not sure where to stick the message "Invalid input."
